##### Primary Counting Method

When the situation is as clean as it is in Figure 6.2.1, we can safely analyze the data by fitting the histogram with a function made up of a Gaussian distribution plus a polynomial background. The fitting is carried out using the root\({}^{\text{TM}}\) software system maintained by the Cern software group. The exact fit function we used in Figure 6.2.1 is

\[F(m)=\frac{N_{\pi^{0}}}{d_{bins}w_{\pi^{0}}\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\Biggl{(}-\frac{ \left(m-m_{\pi^{0}}\right)^{2}}{2w_{\pi^{0}}^{2}}\Biggr{)}+\sum_{i=0}^{3}c_{i} m^{i}\, \tag{6.2.2}\]

where \(d_{bins}\) is the density of bins in the histogram, \(N_{\pi^{0}}\), \(w_{\pi^{0}}\), and \(m_{\pi^{0}}\) are the parameters for the \(\pi^{0}\) counting (number, width, and mass, respectively), and the last term is a 3\({}^{\text{rd}}\) order polynomial background function. This sort of fit can have its systematic error contribution approximated by analyzing its dependence on the background function. In this case, the background function can easily be modified under the peak by extending or reducing the range of the fit. For this particular channel, as a result of modifying the parameters of the fit, we observed the number of \(\pi^{0}\)s range from 3.83x10\({}^{4}\) to 4.16x10\({}^{4}\). Thus, the number of counts for this histogram would be 4.00x10\({}^{4}\) with about 1% statistical error, and 5% systematic uncertainty.

